     "rate": "+0%",
     "text": "In nineteen eighty-three, a seventeen year old needed three dollars and a paper ID to grab a cold beer. Teens think sneaking a drink was harder back then. Old paper IDs didn't have holograms, chips, or tamper-proof plastic. Anyone with a basic typewriter could alter a birth date in minutes. Before nineteen eighty-four, several American states legally permitted eighteen year olds to buy beer. So store cashiers rarely even checked who was buying. Then the nineteen eighty-four Drinking Age Act forced every state to set the limit at twenty-one. Today, retail scanners read encoded PDF417 barcodes to spot forged dates instantly. The analog beer run was just built on simpler rules."
